  <sys:String x:Key="SectionShaders">Shaders</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Precompile">Precompile</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="PrecompileOn">Compile pipelines of earlier runs while loading (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="PrecompileHint">DXUP remembers which shader combinations the game needs and, from the second start on, compiles them in the background as soon as the game loads the shaders. Fewer stutters, nothing is missing.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AsyncShaders">Shader compilation</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AsyncOn">In the background (async)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AsyncHint">Fewer stutters when the game needs new shaders. Whatever needs a shader that is not ready yet is missing for a moment instead (usually a few frames); in rare cases something the game draws only once, while loading, stays missing until it is drawn again.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptGame">As the game sets</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptOff">Off</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptOn">On</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptScopeGeometry">3D geometry (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptScopeAll">All draws</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptSgssaaFull">Full (very expensive)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptSgssaaAlpha">Alpha-tested only – foliage, fences (cheap)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SgssaaHint">Supersampling works where MSAA is active.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Upscaler">Upscaler instead of TAA</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="UpscalerHint">Replaces the game's temporal anti-aliasing (built in: Kena). Set TAA and a resolution scale below 100 % in the game. DLSS needs an NVIDIA RTX graphics card.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="UpscalerAuto">Find other games' TAA pass automatically; without TAA, DXUP's own anti-aliasing (experimental)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="UpscalerInject">Force DXUP's own temporal anti-aliasing (games without TAA, experimental)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SpatialUpscale">Scale a lower game resolution up to the window size (borderless window)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Adapter">Graphics card</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AdapterHint">The GPU DXUP renders on, whichever the game picks. For PCs with integrated and dedicated graphics.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SpoofVendor">Report GPU as</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SpoofVendorHint">The game sees a GPU of this maker (name, ids, driver version) while DXUP keeps rendering on the real one. For games that refuse a GPU or take other paths per maker.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptRealGpu">Real GPU</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RenderScale">Render resolution</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RenderScaleHint">The game renders at this share of the resolution, DXUP scales up. Only for games that take their size from the swap chain.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AppliesNextStart">Changes are saved right away and apply the next time the game starts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnLocalConf">There is a dxup.conf in the game's folder. It takes precedence over these settings.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnAntiCheat">Anti-cheat software was found in the game ({0}). DXUP can keep the game from starting or get your account banned.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnBattleNet">Battle.net games have their own anti-cheat software. DXUP can keep the game from starting or get your account banned.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnD3D12">The game uses Direct3D 12. DXUP only affects Direct3D 11.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnNotInstalled">The manager does not run from a DXUP installation. Games find these settings once DXUP is installed with the setup.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnRunning">The game is running. Changed DLLs and settings apply after the game restarts.</sys:String>
