  <sys:String x:Key="SectionMemory">Memory</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AddressSpace">Address space</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LaaOn">4 GB instead of 2 GB (32-bit game)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LaaHint">For 32-bit games with graphics errors or crashes from running out of memory. DXUP Manager marks the game's EXE as large address aware; the original stays next to it as .dxup-laa-backup and comes back when DXUP is removed from the game. After an update or a file check of the game, Update sets the mark again. If the game no longer starts (copy protection), switch it off again.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LaaOwn">The game uses 4 GB already.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LaaSteamDrm">Not possible: the EXE is protected by Steam DRM and does not start once changed (Steam reports "Application load error 3:0000065432").</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LaaAntiCheat">Not for games with anti-cheat: a changed EXE can get the game flagged.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ErrLaa">The game's EXE could not be changed:
{0}

Is the game still running?</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Sharpness">Upscaling sharpness</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="CustomGpu">Custom GPU identity</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="CustomVendor">Vendor ID</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="CustomDevice">Device ID</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="CustomDesc">Name</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="CustomGpuHint">Hexadecimal (e.g. 10de and 2684) and a name the game sees instead of the real ones; they take precedence over "Report GPU as". Empty: off.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SectionLegacy">Direct3D 7–9</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="FloatEmulation">0 × infinity in shaders</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptAuto">Automatic (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptFloatStrict">Strict as NVIDIA (multiplications too)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptFloatIeee">IEEE (NaN, no emulation)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="FloatEmulationHint">Direct3D 9 computes 0 × infinity as 0, Direct3D 12 as NaN. Automatic suits almost every game; try "Strict" for black or colourful spots, "IEEE" only to compare.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="NanDepth">Vertices with NaN depth</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptNanAuto">Automatic (as the GPU's driver)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptNanDiscard">Not drawn (as NVIDIA)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptNanZero">Drawn at depth 0 (as Intel)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ShaderCompiler">Shader compiler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptCompilerDxup">DXUP (fast, recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptCompilerFxc">Microsoft fxc (slow, to compare)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LegacyHint">Applies only to games using Direct3D 7, 8 or 9.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SectionAdvanced">Advanced</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="AdvancedHint">For tests and troubleshooting. The defaults suit almost every game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SubmitThread">Submit thread</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SubmitThreadOn">Hand command lists to the GPU on a thread of their own (experimental)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SubmitThreadHint">Helps when DXUP's worker thread limits the frame rate: 5–12 % less time per draw in the draw benchmark. Games the GPU limits do not change.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RecordThread">Record thread</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RecordThreadOn">Record command lists on a second thread (experimental)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RecordThreadHint">About 4 % slower on NVIDIA – only for tests on other GPUs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="BackBuffer">Back buffer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DirectBackBufferOn">Draw straight into the swap chain's back buffers (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DirectBackBufferHint">Off: the game draws into a copy that DXUP copies at every Present. Only try it for image errors.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OutputMasks">Shader outputs</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OutputMasksOn">Leave out unused pixel shader outputs as Direct3D 11 drivers do (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="BarrierHoisting">Barriers</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="BarrierHoistingOn">Move barriers that wait for nothing earlier (recommended)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RelaxedUav">UAV barriers</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptUavRelaxed">Relaxed</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptUavStrict">Always</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="RelaxedUavHint">Relaxed: draws and dispatches of the same pipeline write the same UAVs without a barrier between them, as with NVIDIA's and AMD's UAV overlap extensions. Automatic: relaxed only for God of War.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LazyTargets">Render target memory</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptLazyAuto">Automatic (when first written on NVIDIA)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptLazyOn">When first written</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="OptLazyOff">At creation</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="LazyTargetsHint">As with Direct3D 11 drivers, render targets get their video memory only once the game draws into them.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="SectionDiagnostics">Diagnostics</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagIntro">For bug reports: press "Record a problem", start the game, reproduce the problem and quit the game. Then "Create diagnostic package": the zip goes to the desktop and the recording goes off again.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagRecord">Record a problem</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagRecordStop">Stop recording</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagSingle">Or turn them on one by one ("Record a problem" takes the log, debug layer, crash log and frames):</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="StatusRecording">Recording: start the game, reproduce the problem, quit the game, then "Create diagnostic package".</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="StatusRecordStopped">Recording stopped.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagLog">Detailed log (logdxup.log)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagLayer">D3D12 debug layer: reports wrong Direct3D 12 calls of DXUP in the log</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagGpu">GPU-based validation: checks on the GPU as well (very slow)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagDred">Track GPU crashes (DRED: shows the last command after a device error)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagExceptions">Crash log: every exception of the game with its call stack</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagFrames">Save frames: an image every 15 seconds (the last four stay) and one frame's calls in the log (for rendering errors)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagLayerMissing">The D3D12 debug layer is not installed: Windows Settings › System › Optional features › add "Graphics Tools".</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagInactive">The switches work only while DXUP is active for the game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagPackage">Create diagnostic package (zip on the desktop)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagOpenFolder">Open game folder (logs)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagRenderDoc">Start in RenderDoc</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagRenderDocGet">Download RenderDoc</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagPix">Start in PIX</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagPixGet">Download PIX</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagPix32">PIX captures 64-bit games only; use RenderDoc for 32-bit games.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="DiagGpuDebuggers">RenderDoc and PIX capture single frames as DXUP sends them to Direct3D 12, for developers. The diagnostic package holds the logs, settings, system information (Windows, CPU, GPU, driver) and the game's error reports; user and computer names are replaced.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="WarnDiagnostics">Diagnostics are on: the game runs slower and writes logs. Turn them off under "Diagnostics" when you are done.</sys:String>
